-- title:
--   The integer lattice in $\mathbb{R}^k$ and lattice sampling on the grid $\frac1n\mathbb{Z}^k$
-- statement:
--   This file introduces the two basic objects for discretizing functions on $\mathbb{R}^k$.
--
--   1. **Integer lattice.** For $k\in\mathbb{N}$, $\mathbb{Z}^k\subseteq\mathbb{R}^k$ is the $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of $\mathbb{R}^k$ generated by the standard basis vectors $e_1,\dots,e_k$:
--   $$\mathbb{Z}^k=\Bigl\{\sum_{j=1}^{k}m_je_j:\ m_1,\dots,m_k\in\mathbb{Z}\Bigr\}.$$
--   2. **Lattice sampling.** For $n\in\mathbb{N}$ and $\gamma=(\gamma_1,\dots,\gamma_k)\in\mathbb{Z}^k$, the sample point is
--   $$x_n(\gamma)=\frac{\gamma}{n}=\Bigl(\frac{\gamma_1}{n},\dots,\frac{\gamma_k}{n}\Bigr)\in\mathbb{R}^k .$$
--
--   For $n\ge1$ the points $x_n(\gamma)$, $\gamma\in\mathbb{Z}^k$, enumerate the grid $\frac1n\mathbb{Z}^k$, the dilate of the integer lattice by the factor $1/n$. They are used for Riemann sums: for a continuous compactly supported $f$, the normalized lattice sums $n^{-k}\sum_{\gamma}f(\gamma/n)$ converge to $\int_{\mathbb{R}^k}f$, and this is how continuous smoothing functions are converted into finitely supported measures on the lattice.
--
--   **Formalization Note** `realLattice k` is `Submodule.span ℤ (Set.range (Pi.basisFun ℝ (Fin k)))`, and `latticeSample n γ` is `fun j => (γ j : ℝ)/n`. For $n=0$, Lean's convention $x/0=0$ makes `latticeSample 0 γ` the zero vector; statements about sampling assume $n\neq0$ or pass to the limit $n\to\infty$. Apart from these two definitions the file consists of empty sections.
